            "TOCHeading": "Ecological Information",
            "Description": "This section provides information to evaluate the environmental impact of the chemical if it were released to the environment. The information may include data from toxicity tests performed on aquatic and/or terrestrial organisms, where available (e.g., acute or chronic aquatic toxicity data for fish, algae, and crustaceans; toxicity data on birds, bees, and plants).  This section also includes information on whether there is a potential for the chemical to persist or degrade in the environment either through biodegradation or other processes (such as oxidation or hydrolysis) as well as the concentration or amount of the chemical found in the environment (e.g., in plants, animals as well as in the atmosphere or water).",
            "Section": [
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 35,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Aspargine's production and use in biochemical research, preparation of culture media, and medicine may result in its release to the environment through various waste streams. Asparagine is a non essential amino acid that is widely distributed in animals and plants, both free and combined with proteins. If released to air, an estimated vapor pressure of 4.8X10-8 mm Hg at 25 °C indicates asparagine will exist solely in the particulate phase in the ambient atmosphere. Particulate-phase asparagine will be removed from the atmosphere by wet and dry deposition. If released to soil, asparagine is expected to have very high mobility based upon an estimated Koc of 1. Asparagine will not volatilize from moist soil surfaces since it exists primarily as a zwitterion at environmental pH, and ionic compounds do not volatilize. Asparagine is not expected to volatilize from dry soil surfaces based upon its estimated vapor pressure. Amino acids are an important nitrogen source for microorganisms, and are readily assimilated by microbes; therefore, asparagine is expected to biodegrade quickly in both soil and water. If released into water, asparagine is not expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the estimated Koc. Asparagine will not volatilize from water surfaces since ionic compounds do not volatilize. An estimated BCF of 3 suggests the potential for bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is low. Asparagine contains an amide functional group which may be susceptible to hydrolysis; however, the rate of this reaction under environmental conditions is not known. Occupational exposure to asparagine may occur through dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where asparagine is produced or used. Asparagine is widely distributed in nature in both plants and animals, and is a potential degradation product of aspartame, which is used as a sweetener in many foods and beverages. Therefore, the general population is primarily exposed to asparagine through the consumption of plant and animal products and possibly through the consumption of foods or beverages that contain aspartame. (SRC)",
